Subject: Draft objectives — my thinking

Hi Luke,

Following your ask to think about where my technical capability, leadership and strategic input hold most influence, here's where I've landed.

My starting point is that the platform is pre-first-delivery, so the highest-value thing I can do this year is make the first production delivery unambiguously good and define the standard everything after it is held to. I've written my objectives to sit under yours rather than beside them — you hold the portfolio, capacity and team; I supply the technical substance and the first proof point.

Three threads run through all of it:

1. Land the first production-grade AI solution, built deliberately as the pattern the team reuses.
2. Define what production-ready means for AI specifically — evaluation, retrieval quality, regression, and how sources and confidence are surfaced to users. We have engineering quality controls, but not a shared definition of good for the AI itself. My read is that adoption problems with these solutions are usually as much about whether people can tell when to trust an answer as about the model underneath.
3. Bring engineering and commercial judgement into use-case selection, so early capacity goes to a small number of things that succeed.

Draft objectives:

Commercial Mindset — Contribute engineering and commercial assessment of proposed use cases, including whether the process should be simplified before anything is built. Recommend deferral where value or feasibility is unclear, with the reasoning shared. Work with the team on reusable patterns and reference architectures so future delivery starts from a proven baseline. Establish cost visibility per use case so decisions can be made on cost per outcome.

Customer Journeys — Work alongside the business areas we support rather than at arm's length, using rapid wireframes and working prototypes to test direction with users early and correct course while it's still cheap. Iterate in short, visible cycles with design, journey optimisation and delivery colleagues. Run discovery with Operations and front office to understand where effort and risk actually sit. Ensure the initiatives I work on have a named business owner, a defined outcome and an agreed measure before build.

Engineering Quality — Develop and publish the AI quality and evaluation standard with risk and compliance. Ensure solutions are assessed for usability and trust alongside technical accuracy. Build evaluation and observability as shared platform capabilities rather than per-project effort, giving risk, compliance and audit a single evidence base. Apply AI-assisted development practices in delivery and share what works.

Productivity & High Performance Culture — Ensure every solution I contribute to can be supported and changed by at least two engineers, through architecture decision records, runbooks and a named second engineer with hands-on familiarity. Bring engineers directly into evaluation, retrieval design and tuning so these become team capabilities.

Simplification & Automation — Lead delivery of the first production-grade AI solution end to end, with a defined business outcome and audit-ready controls. Define a genuinely shippable MVP for each initiative and extend based on observed use. Work with the central AI function and business owners to take use cases through governance into operational adoption, with benefits measured afterwards.

T&T Strategy Delivery — Help define and pilot the forward-deployed engineering operating model: engagement shape, the graduation bar from prototype to supported service, and handover arrangements. Provide engineering input into the central AI function's governance and prioritisation on feasibility, supportability, cost and risk. Deliver against commitments and raise variance in advance.

On the operating model — I've worked this way before, embedded across five business units at Close Brothers and running discovery directly with the exec at KIO, where I also led the wireframing and interface work for the risk application. The tension to design around is that it runs on proximity and speed while the bank runs on control and supportability. My view is the platform is what makes it safe: environment templates, pre-approved patterns and a shared evaluation harness are what let someone build quickly inside a business function without it becoming shadow IT. The graduation bar and handover are the part worth getting right.

One thing I'd like to discuss rather than assume: alongside delivery, I'd like a defined contribution to AI product and strategy to be an explicit part of the role — the capability roadmap, build versus buy, and product framing of use cases before build. Happy to talk it through.

